-- ============================================================================
-- MySQL Database Backup Operations (mysqldump)
-- File: answer.sql
-- Note: Run mysqldump commands in your system terminal (Command Prompt,
-- PowerShell, or Bash), not inside the interactive mysql> prompt.
-- ============================================================================


-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Question 1: Creating a Complete Database Backup ('sales')
--
-- Command:
--   mysqldump -u root -p sales > sales_backup.sql
--
-- What the command does:
--   - mysqldump exports the schema (CREATE TABLE statements) and data
--     (INSERT statements) of the 'sales' database as SQL statements.
--   - -u root authenticates as the 'root' MySQL user.
--   - -p prompts interactively for the 'root' password rather than exposing
--     it in plain text on the command line or shell history.
--   - sales targets the 'sales' database.
--   - > sales_backup.sql redirects the output into a file named
--     sales_backup.sql in the current working directory.
--
-- Why regular database backups are important:
--   - Protect against accidental data loss or human error (such as an
--     erroneous UPDATE, DELETE, or DROP statement).
--   - Support disaster recovery after hardware failure, disk corruption,
--     outages, or security incidents.
--   - Provide a safe restore point before major schema changes or upgrades,
--     helping meet Recovery Point (RPO) and Recovery Time (RTO) goals.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------


-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Question 2: Backing Up All Databases
--
-- Command:
--   mysqldump -u root -p --all-databases > all_databases_backup.sql
--
-- What the command does:
--   - --all-databases exports every database on the MySQL server—including
--     'sales', other application databases, and system databases—together
--     with CREATE DATABASE statements.
--   - -u root and -p connect as 'root' and prompt for the password.
--   - > all_databases_backup.sql writes the entire server dump into
--     all_databases_backup.sql.
--
-- Why backing up all databases is important before migrating to another server:
--   - Prevents leaving behind related or supporting databases (such as shared
--     lookup, reporting, or secondary application databases) alongside 'sales'.
--   - Gives you a complete rollback snapshot if an error occurs during the
--     migration process.
--   - Simplifies recreating databases and verifying data completeness on the
--     new server before cutting over live traffic.
-- ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
